    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The treatise treats war as a costly and destructive last resort and teaches how to keep it brief, contained and cheap, with a stress on diplomacy, economic principles and intelligence.</li><li>Its author is disputed: tradition names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-tzu">Sun Tzu</a> of the 6th century BCE, while scholars have doubted that he existed, and the 1972 Yinqueshan slips showed that two related texts bore the name of Master Sun.</li><li>For almost 1,500 years it was the lead text of an anthology formalised in 1080 as the Seven Military Classics, and it has been read by military and political leaders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> and the West.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The earliest known commentary on the text, written by a strategist and warlord in the early 3rd century, comes with a preface admitting that he edited the book and removed certain passages; how much he changed has never been clear.</p></aside>
    </section>

